    {
      "id": 139128,
      "name": "Foote",
      "username": "Foote",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
      "created_at": "2015-04-10T12:44:04.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"AlexxKay\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/a/59ef9b/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> AlexxKay:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We came to a different conclusion in my play group.&nbsp; We took the sentence \"If there are no Room cards in play, reshuffle this card into the Environment deck and play the top card of the Environment deck.\" to be a constant effect, which took effect when the Room was destroyed.&nbsp; (When the question came up, the Entry-Pointed Rooom was the only one in play.&nbsp; Considering the case of multiple Rooms in play makes me more dubious about that conclusion…)</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The sentence is not a seperate constant effect. It is a conditional part of the \"Play this on a room\" action, because there nessesarily needs to be instructions of how you play that card \"on a room\" when there are no room cards.</p>",
